+## 16. Dependency scheduling and concurrency control — sources read 22 August 2026
+
+Grounded for EXP-130 and `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-22-dependency-scheduling.md`. All
+entries are primary vendor/project documentation or the primary paper/post, fetched and read in
+full on 22 August 2026.
+
+| Status | Source |
+|---|---|
+| [FULL] | **Bazel — *Sandboxing* and *Rules* documentation.** bazel.build/docs/sandboxing, bazel.build/rules/rules. Read 22 Aug 2026. Bazel's model: every rule declares its inputs and outputs; the build graph is derived from those declarations; sandboxed execution restricts a spawn's filesystem view to its declared inputs (with `sandboxfs`/namespace backends), so an undeclared read is a build error, not a race. This is the strongest existing answer to "agents clashing on undeclared surfaces": the declaration is enforced by isolation, not trusted. Transfer limit: Bazel's declarations are written by the rule author and checked by the sandbox; Consilient's claims are written by the planner and (today) checked by nothing at admission time. |
+| [FULL] | **Buck2 — *Writing Rules* documentation.** buck2.build/docs/rule_authors/writing_rules/. Read 22 Aug 2026. Same declared-input/output discipline as Bazel, with explicit `cmd_args` inputs; actions are hermetic and cached on the declared input set. Confirms the pattern is not Bazel-specific: two independent build systems converged on "declare the read set, enforce by construction". |
+| [FULL] | **Nix — *Derivations* language manual.** nix.dev/manual/nix/2.28/language/derivations.html. Read 22 Aug 2026. A derivation's inputs are content-addressed; the output path is a pure function of the declared inputs. The strongest form of the same idea: identity of the artefact derives from the input set, so a missing input changes the hash and cannot silently succeed. |
+| [FULL] | **Apache Airflow — *DAGs* core-concepts documentation.** airflow.apache.org/docs/apache-airflow/stable/core-concepts/dags.html. Read 22 Aug 2026. Task dependencies are declared explicitly (`task_a >> task_b`); the scheduler runs a task when all upstream dependencies have succeeded. The DAG is the schedule. Transfer limit: Airflow's DAG is hand-authored and static per run — it is exactly the "hand-derived serial lane" design that EXP-130 A1 measured drifting (1 lane cycle, 14 units absent from lanes, 159 dependency edges ordered by nothing). |
+| [FULL] | **Dagster — *Defining dependencies between assets* documentation.** docs.dagster.io/guides/build/assets/defining-dependencies. Read 22 Aug 2026. Dependencies are inferred from the asset function's arguments (a parameter naming another asset declares the edge) rather than listed separately — the dependency graph is derived from the code, not written beside it. This is the closest existing analogue to deriving claims from an import graph, and it works because the derivation target (function parameters) is machine-checked by the Python runtime. |
+| [FULL] | **Prefect — *Write tasks* documentation (v3).** docs.prefect.io/v3/develop/write-tasks. Read 22 Aug 2026. Tasks declare retries, timeouts and caching keys; dependencies form implicitly when one task calls another with the other's result. Weakest enforcement of the three schedulers: convenient, but the graph is whatever the control flow does at runtime. |
+| [FULL] | **Kleppmann, M. (2016). *How to do distributed locking.* martin.kleppmann.com/2016/02/08/how-to-do-distributed-locking.html.** Read in full 22 Aug 2026. The fencing-token argument, verbatim: "you need to include a fencing token with every write request to the storage service… a fencing token is simply a number that increases (e.g. incremented by the lock service) every time a client acquires the lock." The storage server rejects any write whose token has gone backwards, which makes a lease safe against a paused holder whose lease expired. Closing advice: "if you need locks for correctness… use a proper consensus system such as ZooKeeper… And please enforce use of fencing tokens on all resource accesses under the lock." Directly applicable to EXP-130's failure 3 (killed dispatch holding a claim): expiry alone is unsafe without fencing, because the expired holder can wake and write. |
+| [FULL] | **Burrows, M. (2006). *The Chubby Lock Service for Loosely-Coupled Distributed Systems.* OSDI '06. Google Research publication page, research.google/pubs/the-chubby-lock-service-for-loosely-coupled-distributed-systems/.** Landing page read 22 Aug 2026. Chubby provides coarse-grained locks with sessions/leases and sequencers (Chubby's name for fencing tokens); the paper's stated design lesson is that a lock service used for correctness must let the *resource* check the token, not merely the lock holder. |
+| [FULL] | **git-worktree(1) manual page.** git-scm.com/docs/git-worktree. Read 22 Aug 2026. The load-bearing sentence for EXP-130 failure 1: a linked worktree shares the repository "except per-worktree files such as `HEAD`, `index`, etc." — i.e. every worktree has its own index, so two agents staging in different worktrees cannot race on `.git/index.lock`. Isolation by construction, no claim protocol needed for the index. |
